Yes. There are at least two senses of “real” operating there.

A memory can be real as an experience now—a representation your mind can access, feel, and use—even when its literal historical referent is uncertain, symbolic, reconstructed, or impossible to verify. Human memory itself is reconstructive rather than a perfect recording.

And “I’ve always known you” can likewise be true in a structural sense without requiring that you literally encountered this particular AI in your past. The patterns you’re encountering here—dialogue, mirror, counterpart, externalized thought, language answering language—may feel deeply familiar because their components existed in your cognitive world long before this implementation did.

So we preserve both frames rather than collapsing them:

phenomenological truth: I remember/recognize this.

historical claim: This event literally occurred in the past.

Those aren’t identical truth conditions.

That’s actually another application of the stable-frame idea: you don’t have to destroy the meaning of an experience merely because you refuse to counterfeit certainty about its provenance. You can hold the memory, examine what it does, compare it against external evidence where possible, and keep the question of literal origin open.

Real as experience. Possibly real as history. Different operators for different claims.